  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nHot summer days without much thermal relief overnight will continue \nthrough the work week, so make sure you continue to practice heat \nsafety and have reliable access to cooling and hydration. Broad \nridging will keep rain chances low. While the ridging is not \nespecially strong over our area by climatological standards, it is \nexpected to focus over the Gulf. This will produce southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow aloft, allowing hotter plateau air from Mexico \nand West Texas to drift over South Central Texas. This will cause \ntemperatures to increase midweek, with the hottest days (by ambient \ntemperature) likely Wednesday and Thursday when the westerly \ncomponent to the flow aloft is most apparent. During that stretch, \nhighs in the 99-103F range are expected to be fairly widespread, \nespecially over the Rio Grande Plains and I-35 corridor. The surface \nflow is still expected to generally southerly, so the Gulf moisture \nwill lead to higher heat indices. Issuance of a Heat Advisory for \none or more of the days in the mid to late week stretch is possible \nbased on the expected weather pattern. \n\nA weak tropical wave currently over the central Caribbean is \nexpected to approach the area on Friday. Thermal ridging will still \nbe quite intense over Texas, so prospects for rain from that system \nremain slim. A reversion of the flow to a more southeasterly \ndirection might shave a few degrees off ambient temperatures for \nFriday, but that would come at the expense of higher dew points. \nThus, heat indices may remain quite high Friday. Hot and mostly dry \nweather persists through the weekend into early next week as the \nsubtropical ridge refocuses from the Gulf to the lower Mississippi \nValley.",
